WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.983
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.620
MITCHELL: Let me welcome
you all here this evening.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:30.620
I'm Bill Mitchell.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.430
I'm Dean of the School of
Architecture and Planning.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:38.140
The occasion this evening that
causes this talk to take place

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:42.700
is the opening of a new space
in the School of Architecture

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.410
and Planning that includes
a magnificent recent work

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:52.000
by Frank Stella, the Loohooloo,
made available to MIT

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.850
very generously by Elliot Wolk.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:58.750
We've worked over the weekend to
get it all ready to open today.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.360
And sure enough, things
got finished just in time,

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.310
and the space duly did open.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:07.499
It's in Building 7.

00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:09.790
I had to check this just
before I came up here to talk.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.790
It's room 7336.

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.410
Am I correct on this?

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:15.309
Yes.

00:01:15.309 --> 00:01:15.850
I am correct.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:16.657
It's 7336.

00:01:16.657 --> 00:01:18.490
They haven't put the
number on the door yet,

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.320
so I have to be
careful that I give you

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.330
the right number on this.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.970
It's not open this evening,
but as of tomorrow,

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:26.500
it will be open to the public.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.050
And I encourage everybody
to come and take

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:30.775
a look at this piece.

00:01:30.775 --> 00:01:33.580
It's a magnificent
piece and a great room,

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.890
and it will also bring
you into the heart

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.560
of the rebuild
headquarters space

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:40.360
for the Department
of Architecture

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.820
and the Department of
Urban Studies and Planning

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.670
in the School of
Architecture and Planning.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:54.250
And it also includes a really
interesting, new exhibition

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.190
space that we intend to
make very active use of.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.500
So we welcome you
all to that space,

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:04.240
and we very much look forward
to seeing you all there.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.030
Let me make a very brief
introduction to what

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.490
we're going to do this evening.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:12.040
To anyone who's
interested in painting,

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.350
or in thinking about
painting, or in the theorizing

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:15.940
of painting, Frank
Stella really,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.500
truly needs no introduction.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.100
So I am going to be brief.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.950
Born in 1936, a
Boston area native,

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:26.600
educated at Phillips
Andover and at Princeton.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.840
For four decades, he has
been producing series

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.320
after a series of astonishingly
inventive and thought-provoking

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.620
explorations of what
non-figurative painting can be

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.360
and can become.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.280
He's had all sorts of
extraordinary twists

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.750
and turns in the
directions that he

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:42.670
has taken, confounded
and astonished

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.200
the critics at times.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.840
That has been
unfailingly thought

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.910
provoking and really
pushing the envelope,

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.510
pushing the edges of
what painting can be.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:55.480
In the wake of the heyday
of abstract expressionism

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.300
in the '50s, he began to
produce, as many of you know,

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.220
his black paintings series.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.620
This was followed by the
aluminum and copper paintings

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.480
and the shaped
canvases, breaking out

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.490
of the rectangular plane.

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:09.760
He moved into painted
metal reliefs in the '70s.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.300
Later, huge three-dimensional
pieces in the Moby Dick series

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:14.230
and beyond.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.450
And explorations that's
continued until now

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:20.230
and has given us Loohooloo,
the piece that we'll

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.150
be able to see later on.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:25.810
He's moved back and forth from
rigorously precise execution

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:29.250
to slightly playful
improvisation,

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.970
from straight lines
and flat polygons

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.730
to the curves with spatial
readings of the exotic birds

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:35.050
series.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:37.330
He's been an innovator
in fabrication technique

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:40.900
and in the means of making,
of executing paintings,

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.090
as you will see later on.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:46.250
In his Norton lectures
at Harvard a decade ago,

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.000
he produced a sustained
theoretical exploration

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.680
of the aims and
means of painting

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.190
that deserves reading
and re-reading.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:57.070
We're going to have a
conversation about mostly

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:57.910
recent work.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:00.820
We're going to sit
over here on the stage

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:04.372
and just talk about the
slides as they come up.

00:04:04.372 --> 00:04:05.830
I have to tell you,
it's completely

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.750
unrehearsed and ad hoc.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:09.740
We just looked at the
slides a few minutes ago.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:11.710
And we're going to
do our best to make

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:16.329
this interesting for you,
but I think it will be.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.370
Just before we
start, what I'd like

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.820
to do to give you an idea of
what we've been going through

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:25.510
in pulling this
extraordinary piece of work

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:27.370
into the MIT
context-- we're going

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:32.920
to show you a video of
the work arriving at MIT

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:36.160
and, in particular, some
extraordinarily skillful crane

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.560
operators maneuvering
it in through the window

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:40.972
of Building 9.

00:04:40.972 --> 00:04:42.180
I saw some of this happening.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.660
I haven't seen the video.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:46.060
So let's take a look
and see what happened

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:48.000
as this piece joined MIT.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:01.473
[CRANE BEEPING]

00:05:04.966 --> 00:05:08.459
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:29.556 --> 00:08:33.049
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:41.532 --> 00:08:43.250
STELLA: Thanks.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:44.025
Is that on?

00:08:44.025 --> 00:08:44.650
MITCHELL: Yeah.

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:45.200
We're on.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:47.960
STELLA: Okay.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:50.630
I can't resist a blackboard,
so I'm going to draw--

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:51.410
I'm not going to draw, actually.

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:53.451
I'm going to write a few
words on the blackboard.

00:08:53.451 --> 00:08:57.310
And then we can start
showing the slides

00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:00.170
and go on from there.

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:02.840
They are basically two
questions that I get asked

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:04.490
over, and over, and over again.

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:09.140
One, why did you change, and
two, what's going on today.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:11.600
Why I changed, I really
don't have an answer to that

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:14.435
except it's obvious.

00:09:14.435 --> 00:09:15.600
You keep going.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.120
But what's going on today, which
is a way of talking about what

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:21.830
we just saw in the video clip--

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:25.010
I'd just like to
run by you as simply

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:32.540
as I can the way I see
what's happening, say, now.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:35.060
I'm not going to define
what the art of the '90s is,

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:39.230
but it's interesting to see,
or at least to talk about,

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:43.130
what's happening and not
just what I'm thinking about,

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:45.770
but really what everybody
else is thinking about.

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:48.560
And it's pretty
easy to explain it

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:51.090
or to make a very
simplified version of it.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.540
And so it really
consists of two things.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:10:02.510
And so what you have today
is you have a painting.

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:05.690
So if you have painting--

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:08.360
I know you all know
that that's painting.

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:11.820
But it doesn't have
to be just painting.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.570
It's really art to today.

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:17.510
So because you're making art,
and most of it can be painted

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:19.430
or it could be other
things, it breaks down

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:22.690
into two things, which
is we have literalism--

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:26.440
it's a horrible word,
but it's the best one--

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:29.046
and we have illusionism.

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:37.760
And we used to have
problems like abstraction

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:40.910
and representationalism, but
that doesn't really matter.

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:43.140
I mean, we have
100 years of that.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:47.370
And it's come now, in the '90s
or at the end of the century,

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:50.150
to this kind of way
of seeing things.

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:53.570
So they don't necessarily
have to conflict,

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:58.940
but they represent two different
polls, or they represent,

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:00.950
maybe, two different ideas.

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:05.340
But they all revolve
around the same thing,

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:08.000
which is that in
order to do anything,

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:09.923
you have to deal
with boundaries--

00:11:12.004 --> 00:11:13.670
I don't know why I
like spellings these.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:15.169
I just like writing
with the chalk--

00:11:15.169 --> 00:11:16.260
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:17.605
--to see if I can spell.

00:11:17.605 --> 00:11:20.690
You have boundaries
and you have surface.

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:23.980
And that's what you're going
to see a lot of in the slides,

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:25.730
and those are the kinds
of things that I'm

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:29.090
going to talk about a lot.

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:31.850
What you saw in the
video, you can already

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:34.490
see how much it's about
surface and how much

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:36.230
it's about boundaries.

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:39.410
And you can see that
if it were just what it

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:42.620
is when you see the
back side of the piece,

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:43.940
that would be the literal part.

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:45.407
I mean, that might
be good enough.

00:11:45.407 --> 00:11:46.490
It might be a Henry Moore.

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:49.100
It might be a
minimalist structure.

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:51.440
It could be in steel
and be a Richard Serra

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:52.920
and do quite well.

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:55.130
But it has something
else, which is

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:57.260
the painting, which is the
surface that I worked on

00:11:57.260 --> 00:11:58.110
anyway.

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:01.370
It's involved with
illusion, or illusionism,

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:04.690
and that's what brings
it back towards painting.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:08.480
And the literalism
doesn't exclude painting,

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:12.540
but sometimes it's a conflict.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:16.520
And so we'll go, basically, from
there, looking at the slides,

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:21.510
see how I got to it, and what
other things it leads to.

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:24.830
Basically, what the
conclusion is going to be

00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:27.577
or what you might come
to as a conclusion

00:12:27.577 --> 00:12:29.660
yourself when we get through
looking at the slides

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:33.169
is that art, in
the end, will pass

00:12:33.169 --> 00:12:35.210
through all of these
things-- through literalism,

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:37.930
illusionism, boundaries,
and surface basically.

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:42.100
And it'll end up by defining art
as something that you already

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:44.720
knew it was there, which is
it's going to be painting,

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:46.060
sculpture, and architecture.

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:48.260
So it won't be a
surprise to you,

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:50.380
but that's what
the fine arts are.

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:53.290
And what happens is a lot of
the divisions and different ways

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:56.680
that we have of looking at
things have to do with the kind

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:59.440
of not necessarily
confounding, but they

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.890
have to do with people
taking positions in relation

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:05.980
to these things in which they
want to have their own thing

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:09.250
or find that the thing that they
do best being the better thing.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:11.210
But we're not going to
really worry about that.

00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:13.540
We're just going to see
how they can come together.

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:15.591
And we don't know how
they're going to--

00:13:15.591 --> 00:13:17.590
I certainly don't know
how it's going to happen,

00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:19.330
but you can see in
the work that I'm

00:13:19.330 --> 00:13:21.340
doing that they do
relate to each other,

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:23.260
and they do become involved.

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:25.720
I'm not saying that a
synthesis is a great idea,

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.460
but you can see at
least-- or I can

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:29.380
see-- that there are
some possibilities

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:32.830
for a kind of synthesis
that would be interesting.

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:35.580
And that's why I change.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:38.520
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:41.950
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:43.525 --> 00:13:44.150
MITCHELL: Okay.

00:13:44.150 --> 00:13:46.566
Let's have some slides, and
we can take down the lights.

00:13:53.380 --> 00:14:00.580
STELLA: The piece on the left, I
made at Andover in 1953 or '54,

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:05.209
and it's the Brooklyn
Bridge, believe it or not.

00:14:05.209 --> 00:14:07.750
And a lot of people think that
I painted the Brooklyn Bridge,

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:08.920
but that was Joseph Stella.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:09.845
We're not related.

00:14:09.845 --> 00:14:11.470
So actually, it's a
kind of opportunity

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:13.990
to sort of lay that one to rest.

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:16.192
When I first came to New
York, a lot of people

00:14:16.192 --> 00:14:17.650
said that they were
quite surprised

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:19.090
to see what a young man I was.

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:22.080
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:23.430
I mean, I wasn't that surprised.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:26.130
I was younger then.

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:30.300
And the picture on the
left is a painting which

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:32.700
is very typical for the time.

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:35.340
It's a kind of abstract
expressionist painting.

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:37.880
It seems to me-- actually,
although it might predate Al

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:40.130
Leslie, some of his paintings,
a little bit that way--

00:14:40.130 --> 00:14:41.430
but very much like Motherwell.

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:45.450
Al Leslie, pretty much a
kind of second or abstract

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:47.460
expressionist painting.

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:49.950
But the people that I was
most influenced, probably,

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:54.030
in this painting were
Motherwell and Gottlieb.

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:56.600
And interestingly
enough, it had a title,

00:14:56.600 --> 00:15:01.870
and the title came out of
a New York Post headline.

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:03.370
But anyway, that
painting was called

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:05.440
Requiem for Johnny Stompanato.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:07.690
And I'm sure that no one
here really remembers

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:09.220
who Johnny Stompanato
was, but he

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:11.690
was a hood who came
to an unfortunate

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:16.220
end with Lana Turner's
adopted daughter

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:17.720
when she stabbed
him in the kitchen.

00:15:20.611 --> 00:15:21.110
Okay.

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:21.651
We can go on.

00:15:26.124 --> 00:15:28.040
That was the last kind
of painting on the left

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:30.200
that I made before I
made the black paintings.

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:34.130
So you can see the transition
from the Johnny Stompanato

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:34.880
painting.

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:37.700
And really, it's a landscape,
and the real change

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:40.160
is pretty straightforward
and pretty simple.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:42.889
It happened by painting out.

00:15:42.889 --> 00:15:44.930
You can see that there
was a structure underneath

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:46.513
and that there was
a painting that was

00:15:46.513 --> 00:15:47.810
formed with bands and blocks.

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:53.660
And you can see that somewhere a
frustration occurred and things

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:55.380
were-- and then it
got painted out,

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:57.005
but it got painted
it out in such a way

00:15:57.005 --> 00:15:58.340
that it became a whole painting.

00:15:58.340 --> 00:16:00.447
And that idea of
painting it all yellow

00:16:00.447 --> 00:16:02.780
or painting over the other
painting and making the bands

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:04.520
carry the whole
thing is what made

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:06.560
the black paintings
possible, because then they

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:07.280
were all black.

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:08.780
It seemed to be okay.

00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:10.580
It seemed to be a
way for painting.

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:14.460
And again, I don't want
to tie-in too much with--

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:16.886
but if you were to see
the Mondrian show now,

00:16:16.886 --> 00:16:18.510
one of the things
about the abstraction

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:21.260
in that kind of painting is
how landscape derived it is.

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:23.060
And this is only a
coincidence to me,

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:25.621
but both of the
paintings that you saw--

00:16:25.621 --> 00:16:27.870
although the Brooklyn Bridge
happens to be the bridge,

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:29.360
it's a landscape
kind of painting,

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:31.490
and painting for a
Johnny Stompanato

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:32.930
is basically a
landscape painting,

00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:34.304
and this is a
landscape painting.

00:16:34.304 --> 00:16:36.110
This is called Astoria.

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:39.920
And it was about the
sunrise in the morning

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:42.697
as you travel on the
subway to Astoria.

00:16:48.106 --> 00:16:49.730
The one on the left,
believe it or not,

00:16:49.730 --> 00:16:51.563
although it looks like
an abstract painting,

00:16:51.563 --> 00:16:53.030
is actually a portrait.

00:16:53.030 --> 00:16:55.046
It's a portrait of Carl Andre.

00:16:55.046 --> 00:16:57.254
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:57.254 --> 00:16:57.962
MITCHELL: How so?

00:17:00.801 --> 00:17:01.800
STELLA: These were all--

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:05.569
I did a series of paintings
which were polygons

00:17:05.569 --> 00:17:08.310
with the centers dropped out.

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:10.609
And that could be a frame,
and the portrait, obviously,

00:17:10.609 --> 00:17:11.660
could go in the middle.

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:14.180
But I knew what Carl looked
like, so I left it out.

00:17:14.180 --> 00:17:17.365
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:20.119
And I did a series
of other people.

00:17:20.119 --> 00:17:24.200
I did Leo Castelli and Ileana,
his wife, and all my friends.

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:25.839
It was a group of portraits.

00:17:25.839 --> 00:17:27.589
But actually, it was
an interesting thing,

00:17:27.589 --> 00:17:29.420
because I did an octagon.

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:33.730
Right after that,
the graphics started

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:37.300
to form images which had
spaces in the middle.

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:41.290
And the one I can think of is
a Chase Manhattan Bank logo

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:44.260
which appeared about
six weeks after my show

00:17:44.260 --> 00:17:46.240
at Leo's announcement.

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:49.510
And then that was a
painting done later on,

00:17:49.510 --> 00:17:52.105
which is-- again, and
you can see boundaries.

00:17:52.105 --> 00:17:53.890
It's become an issue
here in surface.

00:17:53.890 --> 00:17:55.440
That's practically
the only issue,

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:58.690
but it's an interesting
way to see it.

00:17:58.690 --> 00:18:02.230
And this, in a way,
relates something,

00:18:02.230 --> 00:18:05.670
finding a way to deal with
the things that come up later

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:10.111
are a way of looking
to make a space.

00:18:10.111 --> 00:18:11.060
Okay.

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:11.720
We can go on.

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:12.220
Sorry.

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:21.680
Well, this gets
into another idea,

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:24.080
which is a way of dealing
with the boundaries

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:25.340
and the surfaces.

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:29.660
But this time, the thing
that's going to be painted,

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:32.690
or the painting, instead of
being kind of self-defining,

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:36.170
becomes something that's
built. And then the painting

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:38.450
takes place on
what's built. So it's

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:40.940
a way of thinking
about the painting

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:42.800
as not so much
marks on a surface,

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:46.160
but as a structure and sort of
building what it is that you

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:48.750
want and then painting it.

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:50.000
But in order for it to work--

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.550
the problem with this kind of
way of working and thinking

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:58.370
about things is that it's not so
easy to make the painting seem

00:18:58.370 --> 00:18:59.680
that it really--

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:02.390
it has to do something more than
appear to be on the surface.

00:19:05.647 --> 00:19:06.980
It's just difficult, that's all.

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:08.120
But it's a problem.

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:10.850
It also has it's kind of
rewards, because it's a way

00:19:10.850 --> 00:19:14.360
to be, at least pictorially
and graphically,

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:17.758
dramatic in a
relatively easy way.

00:19:17.758 --> 00:19:19.710
MITCHELL: And these are
fabricated in metal?

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:20.480
STELLA: Yeah.

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:23.990
That's fabricated in aluminum,
and the other has fiberglass.

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:25.910
And that's honeycomb aluminum.

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:29.272
And the other thing here
is treating the surface.

00:19:29.272 --> 00:19:31.730
It doesn't show up much there,
because it was very shallow,

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:35.300
but the aluminum
was etched first

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:37.520
and then the painting and
then inc and some things

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:40.339
were scrubbed on the surface
to give it a kind of movement.

00:19:40.339 --> 00:19:42.880
MITCHELL: Did you ever get them
fabricated in a shop and then

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:43.850
bring them there?

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:44.391
STELLA: Yeah.

00:19:44.391 --> 00:19:47.210
This was the beginning of the
surfboard technology, actually,

00:19:47.210 --> 00:19:51.950
which ends with [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:54.581
This was fabricated
in California.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:02.630
And this is taking
what we've just

00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:16.650
seen before one step further,
but there are new elements.

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:21.040
And there's an attempt here
to make the surfaces move,

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:23.760
so they're not just planar
surfaces in relation

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:27.090
to each other tipped or tilted.

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:32.926
It's an introduction of another
degree and level of complexity.

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:39.680
Now, I didn't do these.

00:20:39.680 --> 00:20:42.963
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:44.840 --> 00:20:51.020
But this interests me a lot,
and the reasons are down there--

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:54.260
literalism, illusionism,
boundaries, and surface,

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:57.650
and even painting and art.

00:20:57.650 --> 00:21:01.140
The surface here is given.

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:02.880
On the other hand, it's used.

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:06.570
And it's a very complicated,
very irregular surface.

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:11.060
And on the face of
it, we would assume

00:21:11.060 --> 00:21:14.120
that they didn't really care
that much about the surface.

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:18.060
But let's have the next slide.

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:20.250
It's pretty obvious on
the one on the right

00:21:20.250 --> 00:21:23.880
when they go to the trouble of
incising and cutting the line,

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:28.140
and it is a tremendous feel
for the surface and the given

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:28.760
things.

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:31.020
There's making and
controlling your own surface

00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:33.000
by indenting and
making the relief,

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:35.850
and then there is the use of
the relief and protrusions

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:38.520
that are given by
nature and the surface.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:44.400
So it only goes to show that,
from the very beginning,

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:46.009
people who know
what they're doing

00:21:46.009 --> 00:21:47.550
or care about what
they're doing have

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:50.370
a sensitivity to the whole
scene and the whole situation.

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:57.120
And you have to bring your
own touch to the surface,

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:00.600
but you need a surface
to react to in a way too.

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:02.200
It always helps.

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:04.110
I think there's been a big--

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:05.610
it's not a big
problem, but you can

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:10.110
see that the assumption of being
given a neutral or flat surface

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:15.030
to work on is not such
a great gift, as you

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:16.308
can see from these paintings.

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:24.240
Now, this is something
that we don't have anymore,

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:28.650
and we don't have these kind
of complicated architectural

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:29.970
surfaces to work on.

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:32.690
Again, this is a given
surface, but it's

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:36.000
made much like the
surface in the caves

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:38.100
in Lascaux and Altamira.

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:41.700
And again, it's the
question, or the issue,

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:43.830
or the interest in
surfaces and things

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:48.590
that you can do when you're
given an opportunity.

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:51.450
Things happen when
there are opportunities,

00:22:51.450 --> 00:22:56.840
when there are challenges,
and the Roman and the German

00:22:56.840 --> 00:22:59.340
are good examples.

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:02.010
And they also serve as
pretty good reminders

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:06.660
that the degree of complexity
can be quite great,

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:12.150
and problems in dealing
with them are great too.

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:15.495
By and large, in this
case, most of the solutions

00:23:15.495 --> 00:23:18.390
are what we would
call scenographic,

00:23:18.390 --> 00:23:21.870
and we would tend to say,
well, it's decorative.

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:27.090
And those things are all
true, but when there are also

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:30.930
other examples where these
things are taken to heights

00:23:30.930 --> 00:23:35.550
in expressive and heights
that are truly great,

00:23:35.550 --> 00:23:38.530
and they couldn't be
done any other way.

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:40.260
MITCHELL: Here the
surface dissolves too.

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:43.579
If you're down on the floor,
we have a true [INAUDIBLE]..

00:23:43.579 --> 00:23:44.120
STELLA: Yeah.

00:23:44.120 --> 00:23:47.320
But I mean, it's hard to find
a place to look at things.

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:48.820
It's hard to know where you are.

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:50.880
I mean, it's also hard
to know who you are.

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:52.566
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:58.170
But now, having shown
the things that we

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:00.360
saw running through
the paintings and some

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:03.630
of the ideas just tipping over
the top, what happens is--

00:24:03.630 --> 00:24:08.430
again, we talk about change,
which is that, at some point,

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:10.360
you take things
from the outside,

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:13.350
and you deal with them
in a kind of ordered way,

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:15.950
or you make a mark or a drawing.

00:24:15.950 --> 00:24:16.950
You try to order things.

00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:18.570
You try to organize them.

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:23.370
And then at some
point, it's hard

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:25.740
not to be interested
in the things that

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:29.340
happen that are beyond your
control and beyond your,

00:24:29.340 --> 00:24:34.620
actually, imaginative
ability, or just any ability.

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:37.300
I mean, for me, this is beyond
my ability to comprehend it.

00:24:37.300 --> 00:24:41.360
But anyway, these were actually
done relatively recently,

00:24:41.360 --> 00:24:43.240
about four or five
years ago, but I started

00:24:43.240 --> 00:24:44.730
on it when I was working on--

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:47.570
actually, when I was in
Eliot House at Harvard.

00:24:47.570 --> 00:24:50.800
And I was blowing smoke
rings, and Harriet

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:53.050
had gone back to the city,
and I was there alone.

00:24:53.050 --> 00:24:58.950
And they heated up the room,
as they do in all universities,

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:00.930
and then they think
that's warm enough.

00:25:00.930 --> 00:25:02.040
They turn off the heat.

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:04.230
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:06.480
And this was a happy moment.

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:09.720
When the heat was off, and
the air was very still,

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:12.600
and it was still warm enough
blowing the smoke rings,

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:14.230
they started to do
a lot of things.

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:16.830
And they filled up, and I
really could see the smoke

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:19.290
just because I was alone in
the room with just one light

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:20.123
where I was reading.

00:25:20.123 --> 00:25:23.180
And blowing the smoke
out, the way they hovered,

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:24.870
and they held, and
then they did what

00:25:24.870 --> 00:25:26.415
they did here,
which they dropped,

00:25:26.415 --> 00:25:27.540
and they sort of bifurcate.

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:29.130
They make other smoke rings.

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:31.580
And as you can see,
these are photographs.

00:25:31.580 --> 00:25:34.080
Actually, the fellow that you
saw in there, Andrew Dunn, who

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:36.496
kept saying shit all the time,
and he was putting things--

00:25:36.496 --> 00:25:39.600
was the one who
photographed these too.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:41.040
But this was almost
as unpleasant

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:43.290
as putting the
piece in the room,

00:25:43.290 --> 00:25:47.120
because the black box was
pretty hard to handle.

00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:49.590
But anyway, we photographed
these from six sides

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:50.550
in a black box.

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:55.680
And then with the photograph
from each side, we had a cube.

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:58.500
And then with that, we
could trace them, and then

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:00.720
feed it into the
computer, and get-- go on.

00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:01.950
I think we'll see some.

00:26:01.950 --> 00:26:03.221
You'll get a version.

00:26:03.221 --> 00:26:03.720
Oh.

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:04.386
There's another.

00:26:04.386 --> 00:26:05.680
Now, this is a continuation.

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:08.270
There's someone else
blowing a smoke ring.

00:26:08.270 --> 00:26:10.445
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:11.217 --> 00:26:12.550
But actually, it wasn't smoking.

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:16.271
And we're making a piece
of sculpture there.

00:26:16.271 --> 00:26:17.770
And I only throw
that in to show you

00:26:17.770 --> 00:26:21.880
that it's the interest in
things outside that you really

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:23.080
can't control.

00:26:23.080 --> 00:26:24.360
Molten metal is pretty--

00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:26.200
that's molten stainless steel.

00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:28.467
And you can use it, but you
can't really control it.

00:26:28.467 --> 00:26:30.550
Or at least, we haven't
found a way to control it.

00:26:30.550 --> 00:26:34.210
And again, the smoke
is the same thing.

00:26:34.210 --> 00:26:36.310
It sort of dissipates
itself, so it's pretty hard

00:26:36.310 --> 00:26:39.280
to get it to do
exactly what you want.

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:41.970
And so I guess that the
interest of the change

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:46.420
has been in things that have
their own way of doing things

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:48.612
that you can be involved
with, you can participate,

00:26:48.612 --> 00:26:50.320
you can look at it,
and you can see them.

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:51.370
You can try to work with them.

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:52.661
You can try to shape with them.

00:26:52.661 --> 00:26:54.520
But it's to see if
some of those things

00:26:54.520 --> 00:26:57.040
won't give you a way
to sort of spring

00:26:57.040 --> 00:26:58.985
out, to get beyond
where you were.

00:26:58.985 --> 00:27:01.840
MITCHELL: What kind of
smoke is [INAUDIBLE]??

00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:04.610
STELLA: Well, my
smoke is cigar smoke.

00:27:04.610 --> 00:27:05.854
That's not smoke.

00:27:05.854 --> 00:27:06.353
[LAUGHS]

00:27:06.353 --> 00:27:08.459
MITCHELL: [INAUDIBLE]
smoke rings anyway.

00:27:08.459 --> 00:27:09.000
STELLA: Yeah.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:10.499
MITCHELL: [INAUDIBLE]
cigar smoking?

00:27:10.499 --> 00:27:14.440
STELLA: No, that's the
porpoise making them.

00:27:14.440 --> 00:27:15.600
That's a beluga whale.

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:16.643
But the whale, he--

00:27:16.643 --> 00:27:17.630
MITCHELL: [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:17.630 --> 00:27:18.171
STELLA: Yeah.

00:27:18.171 --> 00:27:19.960
No, the ones before were smoke.

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:22.450
And also it was
his form that led

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:25.430
to the shapes in the
paintings before,

00:27:25.430 --> 00:27:26.840
which is the beluga moving.

00:27:26.840 --> 00:27:29.080
Okay.

00:27:29.080 --> 00:27:31.930
I guess we can go on.

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:32.430
Okay.

00:27:34.950 --> 00:27:37.710
That's the computer's version
of what we saw in the smoke.

00:27:37.710 --> 00:27:40.710
It's not exactly those
rings, but that's

00:27:40.710 --> 00:27:42.000
one way we could get it.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:47.797
And then you all know this.

00:27:47.797 --> 00:27:49.380
I mean, I'm sure
you're very familiar.

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:50.546
That's just on the computer.

00:27:50.546 --> 00:27:52.380
But that's an open
net, and the other

00:27:52.380 --> 00:27:54.600
is a solid version of
it, what it would be like

00:27:54.600 --> 00:27:56.126
if it were a perfume bottle.

00:27:56.126 --> 00:27:58.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:00.886 --> 00:28:03.710
And that's a painting on
the right, quite similar,

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:05.750
that has the forms.

00:28:05.750 --> 00:28:07.480
And there are two
basic forms in here.

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:09.620
There's the smoke ring.

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:11.870
Most of those
drawings are versions

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:15.740
of the computer drawings
of the smoke rings,

00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:17.880
and there are
basically two kinds.

00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:19.760
There is a net.

00:28:19.760 --> 00:28:23.221
And then there are some
two-dimensional outlines, just

00:28:23.221 --> 00:28:23.720
drawings.

00:28:23.720 --> 00:28:27.680
And then there is some of the
three-dimensional, complicated

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:29.930
ones with all the numbers,
which you all know

00:28:29.930 --> 00:28:32.360
are sliced versions of a solid.

00:28:32.360 --> 00:28:37.432
And then you tip them, so they
make kind of dramatic drawings.

00:28:37.432 --> 00:28:38.640
The other thing are the dots.

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:40.970
So it was a combination
of the smoke drawing

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:45.290
and dot the colored
images in the background.

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:49.040
And those are enlargements
of four-color printing dots,

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:50.630
actually.

00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:55.580
And on the left, there's
some very unsuccessful,

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:59.060
but sort of what I'm looking
for or one of the things

00:28:59.060 --> 00:28:59.960
we were looking at.

00:28:59.960 --> 00:29:02.901
I don't know how far we'll get.

00:29:02.901 --> 00:29:05.150
It was a question of making
the dots three-dimensional

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:07.760
and, of course, making the
smoke rings three-dimensional.

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:12.080
And those dots are
three-dimensional dots,

00:29:12.080 --> 00:29:16.057
but they're derived
from lumps of clay,

00:29:16.057 --> 00:29:18.140
and they're scanned, and
then put in the computer.

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:19.880
It's kind of complicated
and expensive,

00:29:19.880 --> 00:29:21.890
but it was the only
way we could get chunks

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:23.820
that were what we wanted.

00:29:23.820 --> 00:29:27.410
So that's how the chunks or
how the dots, if they became

00:29:27.410 --> 00:29:29.630
three-dimensional would
relate to the smoke rings

00:29:29.630 --> 00:29:31.260
when they became
three-dimensional.

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:34.310
So that's a whole other problem
that's either ahead of us

00:29:34.310 --> 00:29:35.780
or, maybe, become forgotten.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:36.440
I don't know.

00:29:36.440 --> 00:29:39.470
But that's another
possibility compared

00:29:39.470 --> 00:29:42.050
to the way we're doing it,
which is in a relatively

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:45.950
conventional and flat way.

00:29:45.950 --> 00:29:49.880
Basically, the painting
represents the drawings

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:52.070
printed on paper and cut out.

00:29:52.070 --> 00:29:54.470
These paintings, including
the one that's upstairs,

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:58.170
are basically paintings that
have some illusion in them,

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:02.512
but it's the illusion of a
collage of cut pieces of paper

00:30:02.512 --> 00:30:03.470
that are then enlarged.

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:10.490
So that's the piece
that's upstairs

00:30:10.490 --> 00:30:12.920
when it was in the [INAUDIBLE]
Gallery on the right.

00:30:12.920 --> 00:30:14.450
And then these are flat pieces.

00:30:14.450 --> 00:30:19.280
This is in the Kawamura
Museum in Japan,

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:21.410
and that's when
we were conceiving

00:30:21.410 --> 00:30:24.450
of the pieces as kind
of installed murals

00:30:24.450 --> 00:30:27.230
to relate to the actual
space that was there.

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:36.990
And this is what it, as
they say, turns out to be.

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:39.490
On the one hand is a painting
by a graffiti artist,

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:43.530
and the other hand is
a painting of mine.

00:30:43.530 --> 00:30:45.700
In the end, they're
not that different,

00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:51.030
but it's a way of making
the thing sort of move.

00:30:51.030 --> 00:30:52.530
The issue here, for me anyway--

00:30:52.530 --> 00:30:54.238
I'm not sure what the
issue was for him--

00:30:54.238 --> 00:30:57.420
but it was to make it move out.

00:30:57.420 --> 00:30:59.430
And one of the problems
with the graffiti-- not

00:30:59.430 --> 00:31:02.099
that I think that that's either
a good or a bad painting.

00:31:02.099 --> 00:31:04.390
I don't think it's either
one, but it can be very good.

00:31:04.390 --> 00:31:07.120
But one of the problems
with it is that--

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:09.120
and it's the same thing
that happens with me--

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:12.060
which is that you're forced
to make it on a canvas,

00:31:12.060 --> 00:31:14.830
so it has a boundary,
and it's a convention.

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:16.380
So it's forced to
become a painting.

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:19.960
The painting on the graffiti
was put in a gallery,

00:31:19.960 --> 00:31:23.430
and this kid who's perfectly
capable of handling a spray

00:31:23.430 --> 00:31:27.060
can with a lot of effect--

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:30.570
it would be better on metal or
on the side of a moving subway

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:35.010
car than it is on canvas when it
stops moving, when it's static.

00:31:35.010 --> 00:31:39.180
And there's the
same problem I have,

00:31:39.180 --> 00:31:43.200
because it's just on a canvas.

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:44.610
I make it move as much as I can.

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:45.940
I do all kinds of things.

00:31:45.940 --> 00:31:49.110
But in a way, I'm not
that satisfied with it.

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:50.700
Although I like it optically--

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:52.230
I mean, I like it as illusion.

00:31:52.230 --> 00:31:55.090
But again, I'm still torn.

00:31:55.090 --> 00:31:56.420
I'd like it to be literal too.

00:32:02.670 --> 00:32:07.650
And this was the beginning of
the idea on a public scale.

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:10.860
This is just painting
outside on the back

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:13.844
of a theater in Toronto, the
Princess of Wales Theatre.

00:32:13.844 --> 00:32:15.260
And then we'll see
the inside now.

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:23.600
This was the start for
one reason or another,

00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:26.610
and it's the painting on the--

00:32:26.610 --> 00:32:28.650
that's, I think, a
quarter [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:28.650 --> 00:32:30.400
I'm pretty sure that's
what it is, anyway.

00:32:32.960 --> 00:32:36.695
But the thing about
it is that it works.

00:32:36.695 --> 00:32:40.690
When we looked at the paintings
before the graffiti painting

00:32:40.690 --> 00:32:44.450
and my painting, they had
the conventional boundaries.

00:32:44.450 --> 00:32:47.680
There's something about the
imagery, the smoke rings--

00:32:47.680 --> 00:32:49.430
I mean, I guess it's
a little bit obvious.

00:32:49.430 --> 00:32:52.030
But anyway, the fact that the
circular shape and the moving

00:32:52.030 --> 00:32:53.932
around does the
boundaries, which

00:32:53.932 --> 00:32:55.390
are the top and
the bottom of that,

00:32:55.390 --> 00:32:57.940
don't seem to press
themselves on you in the way

00:32:57.940 --> 00:32:59.380
that they normally would.

00:32:59.380 --> 00:33:01.810
The fact that there
are no side edges,

00:33:01.810 --> 00:33:04.950
that the imagery keeps moving
and keeps circulating, I think,

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:06.580
is a tremendous plus.

00:33:11.104 --> 00:33:11.895
That's some detail.

00:33:15.470 --> 00:33:19.310
And then these are
the balcony fronts.

00:33:19.310 --> 00:33:22.280
And what's interesting about
those, I think, in some ways,

00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:24.489
is that they're
totally abstract.

00:33:24.489 --> 00:33:26.030
There's some of the
computer imagery.

00:33:26.030 --> 00:33:28.400
There's some of the forms that,
actually, you'll see later

00:33:28.400 --> 00:33:29.566
in some of the other things.

00:33:29.566 --> 00:33:32.630
We used everything that we used,
and we just made it in relief,

00:33:32.630 --> 00:33:33.890
and then cast it.

00:33:33.890 --> 00:33:36.710
And the result was it looked
very ordinary, actually.

00:33:36.710 --> 00:33:38.615
Very un-abstract
and very much like

00:33:38.615 --> 00:33:41.420
a floral decorative design,
maybe, from the 18th century.

00:33:41.420 --> 00:33:46.860
But the big plus was it was
acoustically very successful.

00:33:46.860 --> 00:33:48.180
MITCHELL: Where is this?

00:33:48.180 --> 00:33:50.250
STELLA: It's in Toronto
in a theater called

00:33:50.250 --> 00:33:53.640
the Princess of Wales Theatre,
which was a new theater built,

00:33:53.640 --> 00:33:56.400
actually, for Miss Saigon.

00:33:56.400 --> 00:33:59.180
And now, Beauty and
the Beast is there.

00:33:59.180 --> 00:34:02.554
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:05.446 --> 00:34:07.509
MITCHELL: This is cast aluminum?

00:34:07.509 --> 00:34:08.050
STELLA: Yeah.

00:34:08.050 --> 00:34:09.159
This is cast aluminum.

00:34:09.159 --> 00:34:09.969
The same idea.

00:34:09.969 --> 00:34:12.639
Like, the other things
were cast plaster.

00:34:12.639 --> 00:34:16.500
And then this is part of the
theater downstairs in a--

00:34:16.500 --> 00:34:18.279
I don't know.

00:34:18.279 --> 00:34:20.320
Not a restaurant, but I
don't know if it's a bar.

00:34:23.334 --> 00:34:25.000
You can see that the
architecture is not

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:27.610
exactly flattering, but
you can still carry it.

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:30.429
And one of the advantages
of this kind of imagery

00:34:30.429 --> 00:34:32.214
and this way of
dealing with things

00:34:32.214 --> 00:34:34.630
is you can sort of fight your
way through almost anything.

00:34:41.159 --> 00:34:43.139
These are big
murals in the lobby,

00:34:43.139 --> 00:34:46.901
and they look nice
in the photographs,

00:34:46.901 --> 00:34:47.900
actually, in the slides.

00:34:47.900 --> 00:34:49.820
They're not so red
hot when you see them.

00:34:49.820 --> 00:34:51.469
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:51.469 --> 00:34:57.410
They're painted with the most
modern billboard technology

00:34:57.410 --> 00:34:57.950
available.

00:34:57.950 --> 00:35:00.980
There's a transparency
that's read and then scanned,

00:35:00.980 --> 00:35:04.100
and then it's sprayed with
four-colored dots on plastic.

00:35:04.100 --> 00:35:09.080
But it's super durable,
and the machine

00:35:09.080 --> 00:35:14.770
can paint that mural in about,
I don't know, 80 or 90 hours.

00:35:14.770 --> 00:35:17.750
It goes night and day, and then
it's done in a couple of days.

00:35:23.795 --> 00:35:25.920
Well, for those of you who
are architects, I'm sure

00:35:25.920 --> 00:35:27.144
you'll love these.

00:35:27.144 --> 00:35:27.948
[LAUGHS]

00:35:27.948 --> 00:35:32.430
These are the drawings
that we made after we built

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:34.170
the piece that's upstairs.

00:35:34.170 --> 00:35:37.500
And we made these drawings
for a gallery exhibition,

00:35:37.500 --> 00:35:40.620
which actually is going to
open next week in New York.

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:43.110
And the reason that
we did it is that we

00:35:43.110 --> 00:35:45.060
wanted to try out
the idea, and we

00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:48.000
wanted to do what we couldn't
do upstairs, which is to paint

00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:49.410
on both sides of the surface.

00:35:49.410 --> 00:35:53.040
So these a freestanding
sort of murals,

00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:55.770
and they will be
painted on both sides.

00:35:55.770 --> 00:36:01.050
And because we designed it
right into the computer,

00:36:01.050 --> 00:36:04.540
we're able to have
it carved directly.

00:36:04.540 --> 00:36:07.350
So the technology, the
fittings, and everything,

00:36:07.350 --> 00:36:09.619
it's much smoother
and much nicer

00:36:09.619 --> 00:36:11.910
than the piece that's upstairs,
although I'm not trying

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:13.350
to denigrate that in any way.

00:36:13.350 --> 00:36:15.180
But we've made a
technological advance

00:36:15.180 --> 00:36:16.890
since that piece was done.

00:36:16.890 --> 00:36:18.720
MITCHELL: What's the
material for this?

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:20.940
STELLA: It's fiberglass.

00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:24.250
It's really a big surfboard.

00:36:24.250 --> 00:36:26.040
I mean, it's the wave
and the surfboard.

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:27.670
I guess it's the whole thing.

00:36:27.670 --> 00:36:29.700
But it's just really
literally a big surfboard,

00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:31.500
and it's cut into
sections, and they fit,

00:36:31.500 --> 00:36:33.910
and it's pretty smooth.

00:36:33.910 --> 00:36:36.280
There are a couple of
pictures of it later on.

00:36:36.280 --> 00:36:37.988
MITCHELL: What kind
of building machine--

00:36:37.988 --> 00:36:40.066
a three-axis, a nine-axis?

00:36:40.066 --> 00:36:40.940
STELLA: I don't know.

00:36:40.940 --> 00:36:42.080
It just works.

00:36:42.080 --> 00:36:43.470
I'll check.

00:36:43.470 --> 00:36:48.010
It's pretty sophisticated.

00:36:48.010 --> 00:36:50.190
I don't know if it has that.

00:36:50.190 --> 00:36:51.300
We can go through these.

00:36:51.300 --> 00:36:53.607
I mean, maybe some
people like these.

00:36:53.607 --> 00:36:54.690
Anyway, that's what we do.

00:36:54.690 --> 00:36:54.980
Okay.

00:36:54.980 --> 00:36:55.479
Next.

00:36:59.320 --> 00:37:00.880
But again, they stand up.

00:37:00.880 --> 00:37:02.194
So it's pretty simple.

00:37:02.194 --> 00:37:04.360
It's a rectangle on the
bottom and a wavy line drawn

00:37:04.360 --> 00:37:06.612
on the top, and then
you connect the surfaces

00:37:06.612 --> 00:37:07.570
like a minimal surface.

00:37:21.470 --> 00:37:25.580
And that's what it looks
like when it's being done.

00:37:25.580 --> 00:37:29.010
That's the studio, and
that's those things.

00:37:29.010 --> 00:37:31.070
And you can see that it
looks like a surfboard

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:32.819
there in the corner,
and it's pretty good.

00:37:32.819 --> 00:37:37.130
It's pretty smooth, and
it's easy to work on.

00:37:37.130 --> 00:37:37.640
Okay.

00:37:37.640 --> 00:37:38.765
There are a few more shots.

00:37:44.750 --> 00:37:46.852
It's mostly masking tape.

00:37:46.852 --> 00:37:48.560
And you can see how
to paint while you're

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:49.601
talking on the telephone.

00:37:49.601 --> 00:37:52.243
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:52.243 --> 00:37:53.826
You learn a lot from
looking at these.

00:37:58.790 --> 00:38:00.830
And this is the last idea.

00:38:04.197 --> 00:38:07.950
This is when you take an idea,
and you give it to an engineer.

00:38:07.950 --> 00:38:09.430
The idea is pretty simple.

00:38:09.430 --> 00:38:14.250
It was a beach hat that
my kids got for me in Rio.

00:38:14.250 --> 00:38:18.270
And it's a piece of foam,
and there are spirals

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:19.432
cut out of the center.

00:38:19.432 --> 00:38:20.140
They're just cut.

00:38:20.140 --> 00:38:22.620
So it's really cuts in
a two-dimensional plane,

00:38:22.620 --> 00:38:26.880
but it's a beautiful form
for twisting once you cut it.

00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:31.270
Almost anything you do with
it looks great, so let's

00:38:31.270 --> 00:38:32.390
see what we do with it.

00:38:35.250 --> 00:38:37.380
That's the same form.

00:38:37.380 --> 00:38:41.820
And this is being
built now in Antibes.

00:38:41.820 --> 00:38:44.260
We tried to build it in America,
but it was too expensive,

00:38:44.260 --> 00:38:45.930
and they didn't want to do it.

00:38:45.930 --> 00:38:47.940
Well, we found some boat
builders in Antibes,

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:53.200
we found a naval architect
to help us with it.

00:38:53.200 --> 00:38:56.490
And what's interesting
about it is

00:38:56.490 --> 00:39:01.700
that it's exactly the same
from a technical point of view.

00:39:01.700 --> 00:39:03.970
It's exactly the
same as the model.

00:39:03.970 --> 00:39:06.150
The model is a small piece
of plastic [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:08.640
It's cut out, and
that's bent and twisted.

00:39:08.640 --> 00:39:11.670
And so we convinced these
guys to just work for us

00:39:11.670 --> 00:39:12.790
for a few months.

00:39:12.790 --> 00:39:15.720
And they laid out
fiberglass on the floor,

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:16.920
and they made the sheet.

00:39:16.920 --> 00:39:20.760
So the sheet was made with
a diameter of about 24 feet.

00:39:20.760 --> 00:39:23.190
And then the cuts were made,
and then they bent that,

00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:27.470
and then they laminated
balsa wood to that,

00:39:27.470 --> 00:39:30.470
and then laminated the other
surface, and then took the cut.

00:39:30.470 --> 00:39:33.200
So they literally
made it the hard way,

00:39:33.200 --> 00:39:36.550
but it's actually the only way
that you can get the forms--

00:39:36.550 --> 00:39:38.610
that I know of any way--

00:39:38.610 --> 00:39:41.060
that are this complicated
and within some

00:39:41.060 --> 00:39:43.820
kind of reasonable
kind of expense.

00:39:56.070 --> 00:39:58.230
The reason we had to
go to this trouble

00:39:58.230 --> 00:40:02.640
was that it has to be suspended.

00:40:02.640 --> 00:40:06.270
And it's in a place
that's aluminum and glass

00:40:06.270 --> 00:40:07.830
and can't take much
of a load, so we

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:14.370
had to be under 6,000 pounds.

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:18.610
It's roughly a 20,
24 foot diameter.

00:40:18.610 --> 00:40:22.440
And this is the same form
seen in two different computer

00:40:22.440 --> 00:40:27.190
renditions of the same idea
used to make a building rather

00:40:27.190 --> 00:40:28.688
than just a sculpture.

00:40:32.006 --> 00:40:33.305
And I think that might be it.

00:40:33.305 --> 00:40:34.971
I don't know if there's
any more slides.

00:40:34.971 --> 00:40:36.830
MITCHELL: Nothing's
on the slider.

00:40:36.830 --> 00:40:39.400
Maybe we can put
the lights on, and I

00:40:39.400 --> 00:40:40.900
suspect that various
people may have

00:40:40.900 --> 00:40:42.100
questions they'd like to raise.

00:40:42.100 --> 00:40:42.641
STELLA: Okay.

00:40:46.258 --> 00:40:55.096
AUDIENCE: When you say
move [INAUDIBLE] the mural.

00:40:55.096 --> 00:40:58.144
You were talking about
trying to get it to move.

00:40:58.144 --> 00:41:01.945
I was wondering if you can
explain, maybe, what you mean.

00:41:01.945 --> 00:41:03.096
Move in what way?

00:41:03.096 --> 00:41:05.327
I wasn't sure, because
a subway car moves

00:41:05.327 --> 00:41:07.459
in a certain kind
of way, but I'm not

00:41:07.459 --> 00:41:08.990
sure that's what you meant.

00:41:08.990 --> 00:41:11.300
STELLA: Well, the subway car
is pretty straightforward.

00:41:11.300 --> 00:41:15.800
It just moves by you and
depending on the speed.

00:41:15.800 --> 00:41:20.300
But it's not so important
what actually the movement is.

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:24.140
It could move sideways, it
could go this way, up and down,

00:41:24.140 --> 00:41:25.350
and back and forth.

00:41:25.350 --> 00:41:26.690
But it's the sense of movement.

00:41:26.690 --> 00:41:30.830
I mean, most paintings
that are successful

00:41:30.830 --> 00:41:36.260
or most paintings that you care
about have a sense of movement.

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:40.070
By definition, painting
has a serious--

00:41:40.070 --> 00:41:42.290
and it's one of the things
that comes up nowadays

00:41:42.290 --> 00:41:44.150
with literalism--

00:41:44.150 --> 00:41:46.260
is painting is static.

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:47.360
It doesn't move.

00:41:47.360 --> 00:41:50.930
And almost anybody can
make a painting that moves

00:41:50.930 --> 00:41:52.950
or make an object that moves.

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:55.880
And one of the
reasons why film is

00:41:55.880 --> 00:41:58.630
so popular, it can
make a claim to being

00:41:58.630 --> 00:42:01.880
more advanced or better than
painting, because it moves,

00:42:01.880 --> 00:42:05.450
it flickers, and it's better
at depicting movement.

00:42:05.450 --> 00:42:09.180
These things are all relative,
but they're important.

00:42:09.180 --> 00:42:12.900
And I think that the success
of painting in the past,

00:42:12.900 --> 00:42:16.130
which is why bring up the notion
or the problem of illusionism,

00:42:16.130 --> 00:42:21.830
has been a success at being
a static art that implies

00:42:21.830 --> 00:42:24.860
movement in a convincing way.

00:42:24.860 --> 00:42:28.820
I don't want to play with
words, but the literal movement

00:42:28.820 --> 00:42:30.770
within the picture,
the action, whatever

00:42:30.770 --> 00:42:35.030
is depicted that takes place and
the emotional substance of it,

00:42:35.030 --> 00:42:36.440
you are moved in a way too.

00:42:36.440 --> 00:42:39.905
But I think that they
have to come together.

00:42:39.905 --> 00:42:42.020
When we say a painting
is great, that's really

00:42:42.020 --> 00:42:43.160
what we're talking about.

00:42:43.160 --> 00:42:45.560
It seems vital to
us, and there's

00:42:45.560 --> 00:42:48.110
a kind of movement in
an unreal situation.

00:42:48.110 --> 00:42:49.220
After all, it's static.

00:42:49.220 --> 00:42:51.800
And at the same
time, we are moved.

00:42:51.800 --> 00:42:54.829
The interior, our interior, our
consciousness, our feelings,

00:42:54.829 --> 00:42:55.870
and everything are moved.

00:42:55.870 --> 00:42:59.990
So there's these two kinds
of movement, neither of which

00:42:59.990 --> 00:43:02.030
are quantifiable in any way.

00:43:02.030 --> 00:43:04.400
I mean, you can't really
very well describe

00:43:04.400 --> 00:43:06.560
or it doesn't mean much
to say, I feel this,

00:43:06.560 --> 00:43:09.384
or I feel that,
because how do you know

00:43:09.384 --> 00:43:10.550
what the other person knows?

00:43:10.550 --> 00:43:12.560
He doesn't really know very
much about what you feel

00:43:12.560 --> 00:43:13.601
or what it means to feel.

00:43:13.601 --> 00:43:18.530
You can say, I feel sad, but
these are not very exacting.

00:43:18.530 --> 00:43:20.780
And it's the same
way about the sense

00:43:20.780 --> 00:43:23.690
of how successful the
movement is within a picture.

00:43:23.690 --> 00:43:26.130
But these things
still, nonetheless,

00:43:26.130 --> 00:43:28.040
as difficult as they
are to deal with it,

00:43:28.040 --> 00:43:29.780
it seems, to me
anyway, pretty clear

00:43:29.780 --> 00:43:36.108
that they are what painting
both is and is about.

00:43:36.108 --> 00:43:37.834
Well, that was a short answer.

00:43:37.834 --> 00:43:38.333
Sorry.

00:43:38.333 --> 00:43:39.929
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:39.929 --> 00:43:41.804
AUDIENCE: Could you tell
us a little bit more

00:43:41.804 --> 00:43:43.890
of the title Loohooloo?

00:43:43.890 --> 00:43:46.045
And also I know there is
a new biography coming out

00:43:46.045 --> 00:43:47.727
on you by Guberman, and how--

00:43:47.727 --> 00:43:49.310
STELLA: Well, you
all know the answer.

00:43:49.310 --> 00:43:50.154
I can tell you that.

00:43:50.154 --> 00:43:51.810
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:51.810 --> 00:43:53.290
I can guarantee that.

00:43:53.290 --> 00:43:55.710
However, there's a person
sitting four rows back

00:43:55.710 --> 00:43:58.500
here who's are
expert on Melville,

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:00.900
but the title comes
from a Melville novel

00:44:00.900 --> 00:44:02.085
which I haven't read.

00:44:02.085 --> 00:44:04.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:04.410 --> 00:44:07.560
And I make no apologies.

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:11.430
In fact, when we went to
a Melville symposium--

00:44:11.430 --> 00:44:14.220
we all live in our own
little worlds, as they say.

00:44:14.220 --> 00:44:17.460
But a woman came up to me and
was so happy that I had done--

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:20.370
she expected me to
do for Melville,

00:44:20.370 --> 00:44:23.140
for Marty, for what
I'd done for Moby Dick.

00:44:23.140 --> 00:44:25.500
I mean, I just spent five
years working on Moby Dick,

00:44:25.500 --> 00:44:27.416
and she wanted me to
spend another five or six

00:44:27.416 --> 00:44:29.760
years working on Marty to
see if I could resurrect it,

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:34.390
because she felt it was a
very under-appreciated effort.

00:44:34.390 --> 00:44:36.820
But that wasn't my goal, and
it's not going to be my goal.

00:44:39.949 --> 00:44:42.860
AUDIENCE: Do you ever seen
an error of cooperation

00:44:42.860 --> 00:44:47.274
between artists and architects
similar to [INAUDIBLE]??

00:44:47.274 --> 00:44:49.420
STELLA: Well, I don't think so.

00:44:49.420 --> 00:44:50.690
[LAUGHS]

00:44:50.690 --> 00:44:53.418
My experience with architects
has been pretty grim.

00:44:53.418 --> 00:44:55.808
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:56.770 --> 00:44:59.750
But I think yes.

00:44:59.750 --> 00:45:02.180
I think it's possible.

00:45:02.180 --> 00:45:05.210
Basically, you get the
job after the architect

00:45:05.210 --> 00:45:06.930
has done what he's done.

00:45:06.930 --> 00:45:10.400
And so if I want to work with
an architect, I'll hire him,

00:45:10.400 --> 00:45:12.390
but I can make the billing.

00:45:12.390 --> 00:45:14.480
You could cooperate.

00:45:14.480 --> 00:45:18.830
But the problems are nothing
is very conducive to it.

00:45:18.830 --> 00:45:23.670
The whole scheme for building
is so obvious and so complicated

00:45:23.670 --> 00:45:26.360
at the same time
that there really--

00:45:26.360 --> 00:45:29.132
the main problem is not just for
the architects and the artists

00:45:29.132 --> 00:45:29.840
working together.

00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:31.850
It's really a problem
for the architects too.

00:45:31.850 --> 00:45:35.140
Few, if any, architects that
I know of in recent time--

00:45:35.140 --> 00:45:37.220
and Frank Gehry tries
it once in a while--

00:45:37.220 --> 00:45:39.680
are able to change anything.

00:45:39.680 --> 00:45:42.200
In other words, you're
committed to a building scheme

00:45:42.200 --> 00:45:43.040
after the plan's in.

00:45:43.040 --> 00:45:43.539
That's it.

00:45:43.539 --> 00:45:44.480
It's over.

00:45:44.480 --> 00:45:46.160
You can see what
mistakes you made,

00:45:46.160 --> 00:45:49.540
but you can't do anything
about it, and nothing changes.

00:45:49.540 --> 00:45:51.560
Gaudi is the last
one I can think of.

00:45:51.560 --> 00:45:52.790
Nervi was pretty good at it.

00:45:52.790 --> 00:45:55.400
But Gaudi, Nervi, those
people could make things

00:45:55.400 --> 00:45:57.020
happen and make things change.

00:45:57.020 --> 00:46:01.074
Corbusier did it in some
of the houses I think too.

00:46:01.074 --> 00:46:02.990
Architects are no different
from anybody else.

00:46:02.990 --> 00:46:07.466
They don't get it all right the
first time, as we well know.

00:46:07.466 --> 00:46:09.836
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:09.836 --> 00:46:13.160
There seems to be more interest
amongst artists dealing with

00:46:13.160 --> 00:46:17.790
murals or site-specific
works than the other way--

00:46:17.790 --> 00:46:19.203
architecture [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:25.596 --> 00:46:26.470
STELLA: I don't know.

00:46:26.470 --> 00:46:27.640
It probably is.

00:46:27.640 --> 00:46:30.490
It's probably true that way.

00:46:30.490 --> 00:46:34.120
But it's a small world, and
there's a lot of building,

00:46:34.120 --> 00:46:39.250
and there are a few architects
who have a sense of themselves,

00:46:39.250 --> 00:46:41.950
and what they can do,
and what art's about that

00:46:41.950 --> 00:46:45.040
maybe only a few architects can
really afford to bring people

00:46:45.040 --> 00:46:47.080
in to do the jobs.

00:46:47.080 --> 00:46:48.580
There isn't much
left in the budget.

00:46:48.580 --> 00:46:50.920
Most of the work that
gets done, as we all know,

00:46:50.920 --> 00:46:54.490
is from the few jobs
that are mandated.

00:46:54.490 --> 00:46:58.900
The budget mandates
some decoration or art.

00:46:58.900 --> 00:47:00.800
And I've yet to meet
an architect who

00:47:00.800 --> 00:47:02.298
didn't complain about that too.

00:47:02.298 --> 00:47:03.565
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:03.565 --> 00:47:05.690
AUDIENCE: Can I ask you to
explore that a bit more?

00:47:05.690 --> 00:47:09.164
Also the issues of
scale-- the beach towel

00:47:09.164 --> 00:47:11.822
that looks good at the
scale of a couple of feet,

00:47:11.822 --> 00:47:15.915
and then becomes a sculpted
form in tens of feet.

00:47:15.915 --> 00:47:20.680
And then it becomes a building,
and it's 200 feet scale.

00:47:20.680 --> 00:47:24.880
Going from that painting to a
sculpted form to architecture.

00:47:24.880 --> 00:47:27.040
It seems to me that
sometimes a form

00:47:27.040 --> 00:47:29.510
becomes pretty intimidating
when it becomes a building

00:47:29.510 --> 00:47:32.159
or it becomes architecture.

00:47:32.159 --> 00:47:33.950
STELLA: I made a building
for the project--

00:47:33.950 --> 00:47:35.408
I didn't have it
here for Dresden--

00:47:35.408 --> 00:47:39.410
and Phillip Johnson built it
in his gatehouse in New Canaan.

00:47:39.410 --> 00:47:41.810
And it was a
monstrous masterpiece,

00:47:41.810 --> 00:47:43.650
but it was only the
shell of what we did.

00:47:43.650 --> 00:47:46.144
We never worked on a
fenestration or the roof,

00:47:46.144 --> 00:47:47.060
or anything like that.

00:47:47.060 --> 00:47:50.302
But the form, he was able
to build it and blow it up.

00:47:50.302 --> 00:47:51.260
It seemed to work okay.

00:47:56.060 --> 00:47:59.900
AUDIENCE: I'd just like to ask
a question about [INAUDIBLE],,

00:47:59.900 --> 00:48:03.493
because I recall, for
example, in the question

00:48:03.493 --> 00:48:06.070
in the panorama, where
they would rotate

00:48:06.070 --> 00:48:08.817
the stage [INAUDIBLE]
developed to the various forms

00:48:08.817 --> 00:48:10.040
of the panorama.

00:48:10.040 --> 00:48:12.070
STELLA: In Mississippi
and Missouri.

00:48:12.070 --> 00:48:13.770
Well the painting-- Yeah.

00:48:13.770 --> 00:48:14.920
The painting didn't move--

00:48:14.920 --> 00:48:17.680
I mean, the painting moved
as you rolled up one end

00:48:17.680 --> 00:48:19.837
and unrolled the other,
and you moved it across.

00:48:19.837 --> 00:48:22.819
AUDIENCE: What
type-- the audience

00:48:22.819 --> 00:48:24.949
rotated in the sense
of-- of course,

00:48:24.949 --> 00:48:26.780
if you go into a
gallery, you're moving

00:48:26.780 --> 00:48:28.780
so the painting [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:28.780 --> 00:48:31.410
You're getting movement,
so [INAUDIBLE]..

00:48:31.410 --> 00:48:32.110
STELLA: Okay.

00:48:32.110 --> 00:48:33.510
But it's still--

00:48:33.510 --> 00:48:34.052
I don't know.

00:48:34.052 --> 00:48:35.759
There are a lot of
ways of looking at it.

00:48:35.759 --> 00:48:37.590
But the basic thing
is you make a mark.

00:48:37.590 --> 00:48:38.910
You act, and you make a mark.

00:48:38.910 --> 00:48:40.110
And that's it.

00:48:40.110 --> 00:48:40.960
It stays there.

00:48:40.960 --> 00:48:43.470
It doesn't move after you--

00:48:43.470 --> 00:48:47.381
you can't keep the paint
moving after it dries.

00:48:47.381 --> 00:48:47.880
That's it.

00:48:47.880 --> 00:48:48.840
I mean, you could.

00:48:48.840 --> 00:48:50.215
You could make it
slippery there.

00:48:50.215 --> 00:48:52.620
There are a lot of things
you could do, but it's not--

00:48:52.620 --> 00:48:56.330
it would be both
complicated and difficult.

00:48:56.330 --> 00:48:59.880
Although you see a lot of that
in installations and stuff.

00:48:59.880 --> 00:49:03.630
I remember Bob Rauschenberg did
a piece for art and technology

00:49:03.630 --> 00:49:07.800
which was a big, steel box
with mud and hot water in it

00:49:07.800 --> 00:49:08.730
and the bubbling mud.

00:49:11.985 --> 00:49:13.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:13.380 --> 00:49:14.576
It wasn't bad.

00:49:14.576 --> 00:49:15.460
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:15.460 --> 00:49:16.590
And it was certainly good.

00:49:16.590 --> 00:49:18.520
As an idea, it sounds
better than it looked.

00:49:18.520 --> 00:49:20.103
I mean, you get tired
of the bubbling.

00:49:20.103 --> 00:49:22.697
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:22.697 --> 00:49:25.030
But you know that you can say
that paintings get tiring,

00:49:25.030 --> 00:49:26.920
but it just raises the issue.

00:49:26.920 --> 00:49:29.090
I mean, it's a good
way of seeing it.

00:49:29.090 --> 00:49:32.710
The point about painting
is painting also has a past

00:49:32.710 --> 00:49:34.240
and has a history.

00:49:34.240 --> 00:49:36.400
I don't know if it's the
convention that we accept

00:49:36.400 --> 00:49:39.940
and that we like, or
they really did it right.

00:49:39.940 --> 00:49:42.820
Sometimes it happens in the
right way, and it's convincing,

00:49:42.820 --> 00:49:43.840
and it's okay.

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:45.460
And then when you
bring up the issue

00:49:45.460 --> 00:49:47.280
like having the
boiling mud, it's

00:49:47.280 --> 00:49:49.030
sort of interesting
to bring up the issue,

00:49:49.030 --> 00:49:51.810
and it gets your attention.

00:49:51.810 --> 00:49:54.670
But does it carry, and
does it hold as much

00:49:54.670 --> 00:49:57.640
as the non-bubbling
works like that?

00:50:00.340 --> 00:50:01.940
AUDIENCE: I noticed
in working space.

00:50:01.940 --> 00:50:03.960
You're very interested
in Caravaggio.

00:50:03.960 --> 00:50:07.670
And now we see these frescoes
in palaces, and churches,

00:50:07.670 --> 00:50:11.830
and things like this, in the
theater in Toronto, where you

00:50:11.830 --> 00:50:13.640
enter, the building decoration.

00:50:13.640 --> 00:50:16.520
Do you find that you end up
wishing that things looked more

00:50:16.520 --> 00:50:19.460
like they did in the
1600s, say, instead

00:50:19.460 --> 00:50:23.324
of the clean, modernist
look from, say, 10-250?

00:50:23.324 --> 00:50:26.450
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:26.450 --> 00:50:28.347
STELLA: What's 10-250.

00:50:28.347 --> 00:50:29.180
MITCHELL: This room.

00:50:29.180 --> 00:50:29.790
STELLA: Oh, this room?

00:50:29.790 --> 00:50:30.236
MITCHELL: Yeah.

00:50:30.236 --> 00:50:30.735
STELLA: Oh.

00:50:36.840 --> 00:50:39.190
I'm not sure.

00:50:39.190 --> 00:50:40.604
What was the point, though?

00:50:40.604 --> 00:50:42.270
What does Caravaggio
have to do with it?

00:50:42.270 --> 00:50:43.875
AUDIENCE: Go back
to that period a lot

00:50:43.875 --> 00:50:46.210
and refer to it as
sort of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:50:46.210 --> 00:50:48.430
STELLA: Yeah.

00:50:48.430 --> 00:50:50.160
The reasons for
Caravaggio are different

00:50:50.160 --> 00:50:53.910
from the general reason,
which is the Baroque.

00:50:53.910 --> 00:50:56.820
The reason I was drawn
to it or interested in it

00:50:56.820 --> 00:51:02.710
was a matter of change
in my working life,

00:51:02.710 --> 00:51:05.610
as it were, for 10 to 15 years.

00:51:05.610 --> 00:51:10.170
Making things, quote
"literal, direct, and simple"

00:51:10.170 --> 00:51:12.720
seemed to have a
tremendous virtue.

00:51:12.720 --> 00:51:19.070
And I didn't find it convincing,
and it's nice to look to some.

00:51:19.070 --> 00:51:20.750
It wasn't just what
everybody said.

00:51:20.750 --> 00:51:24.460
It wasn't all that great.

00:51:24.460 --> 00:51:26.710
And it wasn't, but I don't
think that's as important

00:51:26.710 --> 00:51:29.839
as it wasn't going anywhere.

00:51:29.839 --> 00:51:32.130
And I'm not saying that this
way of working or anything

00:51:32.130 --> 00:51:34.070
is necessarily going anywhere.

00:51:34.070 --> 00:51:38.760
It is different, and to me
anyway, the painting in itself

00:51:38.760 --> 00:51:41.370
and the way it relates
to both the architecture

00:51:41.370 --> 00:51:43.200
and the sculpture
seems to me, anyway,

00:51:43.200 --> 00:51:45.180
a pretty fruitful
way of working.

00:51:45.180 --> 00:51:48.960
And if I look at what I've
done and what I'm doing,

00:51:48.960 --> 00:51:51.190
I have to say, well, it
may not be that great,

00:51:51.190 --> 00:51:54.203
but it's not boring me to death.

00:51:57.041 --> 00:51:57.987
MITCHELL: [INAUDIBLE]

00:51:57.987 --> 00:51:58.933
AUDIENCE: I have two questions.

00:51:58.933 --> 00:52:00.330
One has to do with
transformation

00:52:00.330 --> 00:52:02.150
from the gray
values to the color?

00:52:02.150 --> 00:52:05.306
Because [INAUDIBLE] gray
value [INAUDIBLE] to color,

00:52:05.306 --> 00:52:07.194
and I was wondering
how you did that.

00:52:07.194 --> 00:52:11.130
And two, I was wondering how
the smoke was-- actually,

00:52:11.130 --> 00:52:12.022
it moved vertically.

00:52:12.022 --> 00:52:15.778
And at least the piece that we
had here had two [INAUDIBLE],,

00:52:15.778 --> 00:52:18.105
and it's actually [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:18.105 --> 00:52:20.852
It moves in and out
but not up and down.

00:52:20.852 --> 00:52:24.230
I was curious about
that-- those two things.

00:52:24.230 --> 00:52:26.230
STELLA: I'm not sure
exactly what you're saying,

00:52:26.230 --> 00:52:27.480
but one of the things is true.

00:52:27.480 --> 00:52:30.780
One of the senses that
there's no way of getting,

00:52:30.780 --> 00:52:32.530
which is really beautiful
about the smoke,

00:52:32.530 --> 00:52:35.020
is that, first of all,
it goes in the direction

00:52:35.020 --> 00:52:36.520
that you send it.

00:52:36.520 --> 00:52:38.830
But then when it
hovers, when it stops,

00:52:38.830 --> 00:52:42.020
it then creates its own
sense of dropping out.

00:52:42.020 --> 00:52:44.351
And as it drops,
it forms, again,

00:52:44.351 --> 00:52:46.600
depending on all kinds of
things like the temperature,

00:52:46.600 --> 00:52:48.970
and currents, and air currents.

00:52:48.970 --> 00:52:53.020
And that has its own kind of
really compelling dynamism,

00:52:53.020 --> 00:52:54.181
which I like.

00:52:54.181 --> 00:52:55.930
I'm not going to get
that in the painting.

00:52:55.930 --> 00:52:59.200
I'm not worried about.

00:52:59.200 --> 00:53:03.760
But the idea is that
maybe, in some way, which

00:53:03.760 --> 00:53:07.420
is not that clear,
one way or the other,

00:53:07.420 --> 00:53:08.710
I get something like that.

00:53:08.710 --> 00:53:10.440
That's what I'm
going to settle for,

00:53:10.440 --> 00:53:12.550
and I'll be quite happy
if I get a little closer.

00:53:17.351 --> 00:53:18.976
AUDIENCE: Can you
describe your methods

00:53:18.976 --> 00:53:20.650
of working with assistants?

00:53:20.650 --> 00:53:22.500
What do they do,
what did they do,

00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:23.747
and how do you feel about it?

00:53:23.747 --> 00:53:25.580
STELLA: Well, I can
tell you what they feel.

00:53:25.580 --> 00:53:26.788
They feel they do everything.

00:53:26.788 --> 00:53:28.310
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:28.790 --> 00:53:29.290
And I--

00:53:29.290 --> 00:53:30.390
MITCHELL: They keep
working on the photo.

00:53:30.390 --> 00:53:32.150
STELLA: Right, right, right.

00:53:32.150 --> 00:53:34.940
And I make a mess of all
their really good ideas,

00:53:34.940 --> 00:53:38.532
but you have to learn
to live with it.

00:53:38.532 --> 00:53:40.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:42.730 --> 00:53:44.780
They all are
tremendously talented,

00:53:44.780 --> 00:53:48.244
they do a really good job,
and they do things I can't do.

00:53:48.244 --> 00:53:49.660
And they have a
lot of good ideas,

00:53:49.660 --> 00:53:51.660
but I like some of my ideas too.

00:53:51.660 --> 00:53:53.370
You just work it out.

00:53:53.370 --> 00:53:56.270
You just keep banging away.

00:53:56.270 --> 00:53:59.150
The biggest thing-- the
only reason that they stay

00:53:59.150 --> 00:54:01.310
is because of the opportunities.

00:54:01.310 --> 00:54:04.280
Because we do work on new
ideas, there are things to do,

00:54:04.280 --> 00:54:06.230
and they can get to
run off by themselves

00:54:06.230 --> 00:54:09.530
and do what they want to do
that they couldn't do otherwise.

00:54:09.530 --> 00:54:11.840
But if it weren't for that,
they would be long gone.

00:54:11.840 --> 00:54:12.951
I guarantee you.

00:54:12.951 --> 00:54:14.394
[LAUGHTER]

00:54:16.640 --> 00:54:18.723
AUDIENCE: I'm curious about
these sculptural forms

00:54:18.723 --> 00:54:22.254
that you're working with
now, from the last few slides

00:54:22.254 --> 00:54:23.988
that you showed.

00:54:23.988 --> 00:54:29.620
And they seem very compelling
forms, but in the slides,

00:54:29.620 --> 00:54:31.322
they weren't yet painted.

00:54:31.322 --> 00:54:33.030
One of them was turned
into architecture.

00:54:33.030 --> 00:54:34.290
[INAUDIBLE]

00:54:34.290 --> 00:54:35.790
STELLA: The one
that was painted was

00:54:35.790 --> 00:54:37.998
the way it's supposed to
be, as far as I'm concerned.

00:54:37.998 --> 00:54:40.050
The client would like
it to be more colorful.

00:54:40.050 --> 00:54:42.270
His decorator, his
color consultant,

00:54:42.270 --> 00:54:44.900
told him that it should
be gold with pastel tints.

00:54:44.900 --> 00:54:46.190
[LAUGHTER]

00:54:47.480 --> 00:54:52.050
But we made it white
with color on the edges.

00:54:52.050 --> 00:54:53.880
And Kevin Roach said--

00:54:53.880 --> 00:54:56.872
he made me swear on the Bible
that it could only be white.

00:54:56.872 --> 00:54:58.580
I couldn't ruin the
space and everything.

00:54:58.580 --> 00:55:00.300
It just can't have
color in there.

00:55:00.300 --> 00:55:01.680
It just has to be pure white.

00:55:01.680 --> 00:55:02.490
And I said, fine.

00:55:02.490 --> 00:55:05.610
I want it to be white.

00:55:05.610 --> 00:55:07.830
So then the client comes
in, and he says, you know,

00:55:07.830 --> 00:55:10.290
Kevin says it really should
be very colorful there.

00:55:10.290 --> 00:55:12.210
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:12.210 --> 00:55:14.640
So anyway, it's a compromise.

00:55:14.640 --> 00:55:18.320
It has color on the edges
and white on 80% of it.

00:55:18.320 --> 00:55:22.929
But the form-- that
piece should be white.

00:55:22.929 --> 00:55:23.470
I don't know.

00:55:23.470 --> 00:55:25.678
I mean, I could work on it
more, and I probably will.

00:55:25.678 --> 00:55:28.450
The color on the edges
is not the wrong idea.

00:55:28.450 --> 00:55:30.870
It's just it's not that
easy to get it right

00:55:30.870 --> 00:55:33.030
and get it to
Singapore in 20 days.

00:55:33.030 --> 00:55:36.440
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:37.700 --> 00:55:41.130
AUDIENCE: With some of these
forms, you made the form.

00:55:41.130 --> 00:55:43.487
And then you then paint.

00:55:43.487 --> 00:55:44.070
STELLA: Right.

00:55:44.070 --> 00:55:47.301
But that form, it
could be in a painting,

00:55:47.301 --> 00:55:49.300
or it could be a relief,
or something like that.

00:55:49.300 --> 00:55:52.460
But right now, that form seems
to be pretty straightforward.

00:55:52.460 --> 00:55:54.310
It's a sculptural
form, and it works.

00:55:54.310 --> 00:55:56.620
I think it's
tremendous, and I think

00:55:56.620 --> 00:56:00.460
it'll work as an
architectural form too.

00:56:00.460 --> 00:56:02.110
I mean, I know it will.

00:56:02.110 --> 00:56:05.020
It's just a question
of the expense.

00:56:05.020 --> 00:56:07.960
Building it architecturally
is difficult,

00:56:07.960 --> 00:56:09.550
but the real
problem is not that.

00:56:09.550 --> 00:56:13.000
The forms could be
built. If it were

00:56:13.000 --> 00:56:15.640
for a bandshell
in Rio de Janeiro,

00:56:15.640 --> 00:56:17.260
you could build
it in 10 minutes.

00:56:17.260 --> 00:56:20.770
But if you're going to
put it in Bangor, Maine,

00:56:20.770 --> 00:56:22.150
you've got to seal it.

00:56:22.150 --> 00:56:23.620
It's got to be weather tight.

00:56:23.620 --> 00:56:26.720
It's a pretty severe test
for those kind of forms.

00:56:29.193 --> 00:56:31.067
AUDIENCE: I have a
question about your values

00:56:31.067 --> 00:56:34.420
in the diagram on
the blackboard.

00:56:34.420 --> 00:56:37.030
It seems to me, especially
in the Phillip piece,

00:56:37.030 --> 00:56:39.370
boundaries are, in fact,
part of the illusion

00:56:39.370 --> 00:56:43.000
in the room, that you have the
actual, physical boundaries

00:56:43.000 --> 00:56:47.920
of the four sides to the walls
and that the painting then

00:56:47.920 --> 00:56:50.350
actually starts to create
this illusion of where

00:56:50.350 --> 00:56:51.790
the boundaries are.

00:56:51.790 --> 00:56:54.850
So is, in fact,
boundaries just as much

00:56:54.850 --> 00:56:56.637
a part of the illusion
as the surface is

00:56:56.637 --> 00:56:57.970
and not part of the [INAUDIBLE]?

00:56:57.970 --> 00:57:01.450
STELLA: I'm with
you 100%, or 150%.

00:57:01.450 --> 00:57:04.630
That, I think, is the point
that I most wanted to make.

00:57:07.210 --> 00:57:09.550
It's the relation of
the things to each other

00:57:09.550 --> 00:57:13.630
and their ability to
expand and to form in a way

00:57:13.630 --> 00:57:17.200
as something else to change
the space, to change things,

00:57:17.200 --> 00:57:19.900
to change your perception of
it and how you feel about it.

00:57:23.732 --> 00:57:34.330
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:57:34.330 --> 00:57:36.550
STELLA: Well, some of
those paintings I showed

00:57:36.550 --> 00:57:38.864
were paintings on
flat surfaces, and I

00:57:38.864 --> 00:57:40.030
think they were pretty good.

00:57:40.030 --> 00:57:42.040
But they have a problem
that they are not

00:57:42.040 --> 00:57:44.170
so good unless they
have a lot of shadow,

00:57:44.170 --> 00:57:45.340
a lot of illusionism.

00:57:45.340 --> 00:57:49.390
And the trompe l'oeil is
sort of against my religion,

00:57:49.390 --> 00:57:51.300
so it's a real conflict.

00:57:51.300 --> 00:57:55.120
So it was a way of doing
it, and getting the idea,

00:57:55.120 --> 00:57:57.100
and seeing what it
does, but I don't think

00:57:57.100 --> 00:57:58.512
that I would be happy with it.

00:58:02.360 --> 00:58:10.120
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:58:10.120 --> 00:58:11.680
STELLA: Well, I
like to think that--

00:58:11.680 --> 00:58:15.500
I mean, you can't
nail everything down,

00:58:15.500 --> 00:58:18.760
but I'm not seeking
ambiguity for its own sake.

00:58:18.760 --> 00:58:22.360
There's sometimes
things seem ambiguous,

00:58:22.360 --> 00:58:24.070
and sometimes that's a plus.

00:58:24.070 --> 00:58:29.260
But it's not a goal, and
neither really is complexity.

00:58:29.260 --> 00:58:31.451
But ambiguity and
complexity-- and I

00:58:31.451 --> 00:58:32.950
don't think I was
going to say-- are

00:58:32.950 --> 00:58:36.930
pretty necessary ingredients.

00:58:36.930 --> 00:58:45.705
So the question is to deal
with them and have them.

00:58:45.705 --> 00:58:46.582
That's the job.

00:58:46.582 --> 00:58:48.040
That's where the
art part comes in.

00:58:48.040 --> 00:58:51.890
You have to pull it together.

00:58:51.890 --> 00:58:53.799
MITCHELL: Let's take one more.

00:58:53.799 --> 00:58:54.340
STELLA: Okay.

00:58:54.340 --> 00:58:54.840
Sorry.

00:58:54.840 --> 00:58:59.642
AUDIENCE: You using the computer
a lot in transformations.

00:58:59.642 --> 00:59:01.710
Does the computer gives
you an opportunity

00:59:01.710 --> 00:59:05.452
to generate continual movement?

00:59:05.452 --> 00:59:07.142
STELLA: Not the ones
that I can afford.

00:59:07.142 --> 00:59:09.919
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:09.919 --> 00:59:12.460
AUDIENCE: You've been spending
sabbatical at MIT for a while,

00:59:12.460 --> 00:59:15.930
and there are [INAUDIBLE].

00:59:15.930 --> 00:59:20.360
Have you thought of actually
constructing the artwork

00:59:20.360 --> 00:59:22.060
on the computer, or
do you consider it

00:59:22.060 --> 00:59:27.779
only a tool for the use in
constructing [INAUDIBLE]??

00:59:27.779 --> 00:59:28.320
STELLA: Yeah.

00:59:28.320 --> 00:59:30.270
I see it only as
a tool, and I only

00:59:30.270 --> 00:59:33.120
really like one
thing about it, which

00:59:33.120 --> 00:59:35.640
is the most simple
and obvious thing--

00:59:35.640 --> 00:59:38.765
the ability to rotate the thing
in space and to really sort of

00:59:38.765 --> 00:59:39.390
move it around.

00:59:39.390 --> 00:59:42.150
The stretching, and pulling,
and all the complicated things

00:59:42.150 --> 00:59:44.490
you can do with it, I can do
that with the model anyway,

00:59:44.490 --> 00:59:46.140
and I can even turn the model.

00:59:46.140 --> 00:59:48.170
But it's nice to
be able to see it.

00:59:48.170 --> 00:59:50.130
It's a very useful tool.

00:59:50.130 --> 00:59:53.756
I was very unhappy
with my experience.

00:59:53.756 --> 00:59:56.130
The reason we started using
a computer in the first place

00:59:56.130 --> 00:59:59.650
was to help us build things, and
that was a complete disaster.

00:59:59.650 --> 01:00:03.390
It's worthless as a tool for
building anything, at least

01:00:03.390 --> 01:00:04.930
on the level that
you can afford.

01:00:04.930 --> 01:00:07.570
So I don't know.

01:00:07.570 --> 01:00:09.960
You can build anything
you want using a computer.

01:00:09.960 --> 01:00:12.535
If you build it, make one
thing, it costs a fortune.

01:00:12.535 --> 01:00:15.770
And if you do that
10,000 or 150,000 times,

01:00:15.770 --> 01:00:17.420
it's a perfectly useful tool.

01:00:17.420 --> 01:00:20.460
But for doing it once, and
changing it immediately,

01:00:20.460 --> 01:00:25.720
and doing something
different, it's a nightmare.

01:00:25.720 --> 01:00:28.340
But also, as I've talked
about with some other people,

01:00:28.340 --> 01:00:29.060
the issues--

01:00:29.060 --> 01:00:30.940
I mean, the thing that I'm
interested in, I suppose,

01:00:30.940 --> 01:00:32.940
is what a lot of other
people are interested in.

01:00:32.940 --> 01:00:35.710
The smoke, certainly, I'd
like to put on the computer

01:00:35.710 --> 01:00:40.435
and then see how it would
change under conditions of heat,

01:00:40.435 --> 01:00:44.310
wind, humidity, and whatnot.

01:00:44.310 --> 01:00:47.350
If you know a way to do
it, you can let me know.

01:00:47.350 --> 01:00:53.390
Since it's all particles, it's
kind of expensive to map them

01:00:53.390 --> 01:00:56.921
or whatever you do on the
computer, put them out.

01:00:56.921 --> 01:00:58.379
MITCHELL: We'll
just take two more.

01:00:58.379 --> 01:01:00.720
STELLA: Okay.

01:01:00.720 --> 01:01:02.610
AUDIENCE: Your most
recent work seems

01:01:02.610 --> 01:01:05.910
to be on the verge of movement.

01:01:05.910 --> 01:01:08.240
What is your feeling about
kinetic [INAUDIBLE],, which

01:01:08.240 --> 01:01:13.780
was done a long time ago, and it
is still being done by others?

01:01:13.780 --> 01:01:14.280
STELLA: No.

01:01:14.280 --> 01:01:18.800
I don't have anything
against kinetic art,

01:01:18.800 --> 01:01:22.990
but one of the
problems with it is

01:01:22.990 --> 01:01:24.520
it's the problem of literalism.

01:01:24.520 --> 01:01:26.800
It is what it is, and
it does what it does.

01:01:26.800 --> 01:01:31.130
And it tends to
lack a kind of magic

01:01:31.130 --> 01:01:33.280
that seems to be
inherent in illusionism.

01:01:33.280 --> 01:01:35.790
But I don't have
anything against it.

01:01:35.790 --> 01:01:36.850
In fact, I own some.

01:01:43.430 --> 01:01:44.780
I think it's possible.

01:01:44.780 --> 01:01:46.405
There are a lot of
things you could do.

01:01:46.405 --> 01:01:48.484
But one of the problems--

01:01:48.484 --> 01:01:50.150
from a practical point
of view, movement

01:01:50.150 --> 01:01:54.320
is a difficult
problem, because it's

01:01:54.320 --> 01:01:56.180
a question of how much
you want to program,

01:01:56.180 --> 01:01:57.320
and how much you
want to control,

01:01:57.320 --> 01:01:58.820
and then what you want it to do,

01:01:58.820 --> 01:02:01.010
But look, it's possible.

01:02:01.010 --> 01:02:03.980
I think that it's
going to happen.

01:02:03.980 --> 01:02:07.222
There's going to be more of
it, and maybe it'll be better.

01:02:07.222 --> 01:02:10.219
MITCHELL: We'll take
a last question here.

01:02:10.219 --> 01:02:12.510
AUDIENCE: I've been thinking,
is one of the main things

01:02:12.510 --> 01:02:16.147
[INAUDIBLE] surfaces.

01:02:16.147 --> 01:02:18.230
But the thing that you do
paint on fixed surfaces,

01:02:18.230 --> 01:02:20.660
those are not going
to move at all.

01:02:20.660 --> 01:02:23.510
But on the other hand
you have the [INAUDIBLE],,

01:02:23.510 --> 01:02:27.590
for I do say, for instance, the
artwork of mud with bubbles.

01:02:27.590 --> 01:02:29.630
But there are compromises,
like, for instance,

01:02:29.630 --> 01:02:33.030
painting on flexible
surfaces, such as [INAUDIBLE]..

01:02:33.030 --> 01:02:33.910
STELLA: Yeah.

01:02:33.910 --> 01:02:35.810
We tried to build a
building with Teflon,

01:02:35.810 --> 01:02:37.357
and they got hysterical.

01:02:37.357 --> 01:02:39.320
[LAUGHTER]

01:02:39.320 --> 01:02:40.700
But lots of other people do it.

01:02:40.700 --> 01:02:44.240
Look, I think it's
perfectly possible.

01:02:44.240 --> 01:02:47.240
AUDIENCE: This one I
think is [INAUDIBLE]..

01:02:47.240 --> 01:02:47.830
STELLA: Yeah.

01:02:47.830 --> 01:02:50.715
I love-- I mean, I wanted
to use mirrored mylar too.

01:02:50.715 --> 01:02:52.340
There are a lot of
materials out there.

01:02:52.340 --> 01:02:54.089
There are a lot of
things that you can do.

01:02:54.089 --> 01:02:57.560
But then the question becomes
how do you program or control

01:02:57.560 --> 01:02:58.190
the movement.

01:02:58.190 --> 01:02:59.970
What's the movement going to be?

01:02:59.970 --> 01:03:02.720
Is it going to flutter
in the breeze or not?

01:03:02.720 --> 01:03:03.840
What's the movement?

01:03:03.840 --> 01:03:10.714
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:10.714 --> 01:03:11.710
STELLA: Okay.

01:03:11.710 --> 01:03:13.570
Maybe there's a confusion--
not a confusion.

01:03:13.570 --> 01:03:14.860
I mean, it's my own
fault, but it's okay.

01:03:14.860 --> 01:03:16.140
Movement is one thing, okay?

01:03:16.140 --> 01:03:17.360
And so it's literal movement.

01:03:17.360 --> 01:03:18.740
It does this, or it does that.

01:03:18.740 --> 01:03:21.760
But the heart of the matter
is not as much the movement

01:03:21.760 --> 01:03:23.710
as it is the action.

01:03:23.710 --> 01:03:26.540
It's what's depicted,
it's what's happening,

01:03:26.540 --> 01:03:28.510
and it's what makes
painting great.

01:03:28.510 --> 01:03:31.420
Whether it's abstract
or real is how

01:03:31.420 --> 01:03:34.450
you perceive that, how you
relate to that, to the action,

01:03:34.450 --> 01:03:36.880
to the movement, to the kind
of movement that it is, how

01:03:36.880 --> 01:03:39.520
it's informed, what it's doing.

01:03:39.520 --> 01:03:41.760
It's not just the movement.

01:03:41.760 --> 01:03:43.790
I mean, the movement is
a way of describing it,

01:03:43.790 --> 01:03:47.480
but it's really
basically the action,

01:03:47.480 --> 01:03:49.690
which is the action, the
subject, or whatever it is.

01:03:52.619 --> 01:03:54.660
MITCHELL: There's no way
to summarize all of this

01:03:54.660 --> 01:03:56.539
if we're just going
through, but let me ask--

01:03:56.539 --> 01:03:57.330
STELLA: Let me try.

01:03:57.330 --> 01:03:57.830
Go ahead.

01:03:57.830 --> 01:04:00.900
[LAUGHTER]

01:04:00.900 --> 01:04:01.706
MITCHELL: I'm not.

01:04:01.706 --> 01:04:05.410
Even for an academic, I'm not
so foolish as to try that.

01:04:05.410 --> 01:04:07.160
But what I would
like to do is ask

01:04:07.160 --> 01:04:10.760
you one final, broad question.

01:04:10.760 --> 01:04:11.940
Where do you go from here?

01:04:11.940 --> 01:04:14.270
What do you see as the most
exciting things to explore,

01:04:14.270 --> 01:04:18.260
and what are the ideas
that really animate you

01:04:18.260 --> 01:04:20.267
right now for you?

01:04:20.267 --> 01:04:22.850
STELLA: Well, the hardest thing
and the most interesting thing

01:04:22.850 --> 01:04:25.814
to me is to keep going on all,
as I described before-- which

01:04:25.814 --> 01:04:27.980
was the point of the diagrams
and everything-- which

01:04:27.980 --> 01:04:32.120
was to deal with fine
art as an enterprise

01:04:32.120 --> 01:04:34.520
and to be able to work.

01:04:34.520 --> 01:04:36.627
But it's hard to be able
to work, or to afford,

01:04:36.627 --> 01:04:38.960
or whatever you want to say,
and also in terms of energy

01:04:38.960 --> 01:04:42.470
as well as all the
other things to see--

01:04:42.470 --> 01:04:46.730
to keep working on, basically,
the three areas, more or less,

01:04:46.730 --> 01:04:50.559
at the same time and to
see where it gets me.

01:04:50.559 --> 01:04:52.100
I guess what I want
to say is I don't

01:04:52.100 --> 01:04:55.102
want to drop any one of
the three in particular.

01:04:55.102 --> 01:04:57.560
And it doesn't matter to me
about architecture if I don't--

01:04:57.560 --> 01:04:59.950
if I have to see
Phillip's version of it.

01:04:59.950 --> 01:05:03.680
But it's just to keep
working on the ideas,

01:05:03.680 --> 01:05:05.930
on the models, and the
projects, and to keeps thinking

01:05:05.930 --> 01:05:06.960
about it I guess.

01:05:06.960 --> 01:05:08.250
MITCHELL: All right.

01:05:08.250 --> 01:05:08.770
Thank you.

01:05:08.770 --> 01:05:09.769
Thank you, Frank Stella.

01:05:09.769 --> 01:05:13.020
[APPLAUSE]

